            <ArticleTitle>Concurrent pityriasis rosea and Bell's palsy.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">bcr2016218069</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1136/bcr-2016-218069</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Pityriasis rosea is a dermatological disease with a well-documented clinical appearance, but less is known about causes and treatment. Bell's palsy is a neurological condition leading to acute idiopathic hemifacial paralysis. Recent studies indicate that human herpesvirus (HHV) 6-7 reactivation may be a contributing factor to both conditions. We report a case of the 2 concurrent diagnoses that supports a common contributing factor and suggests further awareness and research into the role HHV 6-7 may play in the aetiology of both conditions.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>2017 BMJ Publishing Group Ltd.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
